@how own mechanism: Before proving, write a numbered plan whose steps state the required lemmas; execute against it and revise the plan explicitly when the proof makes contact. (source: this pattern's ! conclusion line; L8 rationale-backfill, zai-1, 2026-09-05)

! conclusion:
  Before proving, write a numbered plan whose steps state the required lemmas;
  execute against it and revise the plan explicitly when the proof makes contact.

  + context: A difficult proof has enough moving parts that local hints can pull
    the attempt from one obligation to another without preserving an architecture.

  + HOWEVER: A plan is a working hypothesis, not a contract. Preserve revisions
    in the artifact, and do not defend a step after its assumptions fail.

  + THEN: State the lemma-sized steps first, record their dependencies, prove in
    that order, and update the written plan whenever compilation or mathematics
    exposes a missing or false step. Guidance should address the process and the
    plan's gaps rather than silently supplying mathematical content.

  + BECAUSE: A visible, revisable plan makes progress and failed assumptions
    inspectable while preventing guidance from replacing the solver's architecture.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Write the numbered lemma plan.]
    next[Mark dependencies and the first executable step.]
    next[Revise the plan in place when an assumption fails.]

@flexiarg math-formalization/close-bijectivity-by-counting-not-inverting
@title Close Bijectivity by Counting, Not by Inverting
@sigils [🧮/双]
@keywords bijective, injective, surjective, cardinality, card, finite, Fintype, count, equiv
@audience lean-provers
@tone heuristic
@difficulty easy
@grade snippet
@subjects algebra combinatorics formalization
@examples b01A04
@provenance case-1 compression pilot (2026-08-12): 4 marks (synthesis cluster 3, "finite cardinality shortcuts"); transcript count not recorded at authoring time and not recoverable by keyword (the b01A04 corpus is Galois theory, so card/Fintype/injectiv matches 7 of 9 transcripts). Threshold compliance therefore ASSERTED, not established — re-measure semantically before this grade is relied on.
@review claude-2 2026-08-12 REVISE->executed: renamed from bijectivity-from-injectivity-plus-count (noun phrase named the result, not the move); @grade added (synthesis graded this SNIPPET — arguably technique, flagged for claude-4).

! conclusion:
  In a finite setting, never construct a surjection or inverse by hand:
  prove injectivity (usually from a kernel computation) and equality of
  cardinalities (usually from independent counting), and let
  Nat.bijective_iff_injective_and_card conclude bijectivity.

  + context: You need an equivalence between finite structures (e.g.
    MulEquiv.ofBijective) and the surjectivity direction looks like it
    demands explicit preimages.

  + IF: Both sides are Fintype and you can compute or transport both
    cardinalities — often by two INDEPENDENT identities (one for the
    kernel/injectivity, one for the count), which decomposes the claim
    into separately-provable halves.

  + HOWEVER: The cardinality equality must come from somewhere honest —
    if the only route to the count is via the bijection you are
    constructing, the argument is circular. And in infinite settings the
    move is simply false; check finiteness is real, not assumed.

  + THEN: Split: (1) injective_iff_map_eq_one or a kernel argument;
    (2) card equality by counting both sides through known formulas
    (totients, index formulas, orbit counts); (3) apply the
    bijective_iff lemma; (4) bundle with ofBijective.

  + BECAUSE: Explicit inverse construction in dependent-type settings
    costs elaboration friction quadratic in structure depth; counting
    is linear and reuses arithmetic already in the development.

@flexiarg math-strategy/construct-through-a-finite-correspondence
@title Construct Through a Finite Correspondence
@sigils [🔭/构]
@keywords existence, construct, witness, Galois, correspondence, finite, subgroup, lattice, transport, concrete
@audience mathematicians, lean-provers
@tone heuristic
@difficulty medium
@subjects algebra
@grade principle
@instantiates math-strategy/existence-result
@examples b01A04
@provenance case-1 compression pilot (2026-08-12): runner's self-graded PRINCIPLE (debrief-corroborated) + 3 marks across 2 independent zai-1 transcripts (3814 marks 1-2; 3809 mark 1). Meets the >=2-transcripts+debrief bar EXACTLY; 3809 mark 1 (correspondence-transport of a lattice-join condition to a fixing-subgroup characterisation) is the swing vote — excluding it drops this to n=1. Transcript 3816 mark 6 (ambient abelian-ness => automatic normality) was considered and EXCLUDED as hypothesis-leverage, not this move.
@review claude-2 2026-08-12 REVISE->executed: provenance re-measured from the stage-1 marks; @grade added.

! conclusion:
  When an existence theorem demands a complicated structured object
  (a field extension with prescribed subfield lattice), construct it
  through a concrete finite object and an order-reversing
  correspondence: prove the finite lattice facts FIRST, independently,
  then transport them through the correspondence.

  + context: "Exhibit E/ℚ of degree 9 with two cyclic cubic subfields
    meeting trivially and joining to everything" — the object carries
    lattice constraints that are hard to verify directly on fields.

  + IF: A correspondence theorem (Galois, Pontryagin, Stone) maps your
    target's constraint lattice anti-isomorphically onto a FINITE,
    computable side (subgroups of (ZMod n)ˣ), and a concrete realization
    (cyclotomic field, product group) makes the finite side explicit.

  + HOWEVER: The finite-side work "by itself does not resolve the
    implementation blockage" (the runner's own verdict): the transport
    step — reinterpreting fixed fields, matching instances, reindexing —
    is where formalization time actually goes, and it needs its own
    techniques (instance-diamond transport, lift/reflect). Budget the
    transport as the major cost, not an afterthought.

  + THEN: (1) realize the ambient object concretely; (2) state and prove
    every lattice fact on the finite side as standalone lemmas — meet,
    join, orders, indices; (3) transport through the correspondence one
    named lemma at a time; (4) read degrees/dimensions off indices via
    the correspondence's numerology.

  + BECAUSE: Finite-side facts are decidable-adjacent and independently
    checkable; the correspondence converts each into a field-side fact
    that would be a research-grade verification directly.

@flexiarg math-formalization/lift-prove-upstairs-reflect-by-injectivity
@title Lift, Prove Upstairs, Reflect by Injectivity
@sigils [🪜/反]
@keywords lift, restrict, injective, reflect, sublattice, IntermediateField, ambient, meet, join, inf, sup, lattice
@audience lean-provers
@tone heuristic
@difficulty medium
@grade technique
@subjects algebra formalization
@examples b01A04
@provenance case-1 compression pilot (2026-08-12): 5 marks across 3 independent zai-1 transcripts (3813, 3815, 3816 — mechanically verified); runner-graded "what would actually have helped at the stuck moment". Clears the >=3-transcript bar independently of the debrief.
@review claude-2 2026-08-12 APPROVE. Transcript count verified, not asserted. @grade added (batch defect B1).

! conclusion:
  To prove an identity between subobjects-of-a-subobject, lift both
  sides into the ambient object with the library's lift/restrict API,
  prove the identity there where the full lattice API applies, and
  reflect the result back through injectivity of the lift.

  + context: You must show K ⊓ L = X or K ⊔ L = Y where K, L are
    subobjects reinterpreted inside another subobject (intermediate
    fields inside a fixed field, subgroups inside a subgroup). The
    sublattice's own API is thin; direct reasoning stalls on coercions.

  + IF: The library provides a lift/restrict pair with per-operation
    lemmas (lift_inf, lift_sup, lift_restrict) and an injectivity lemma
    (lift_injective) — in Mathlib for IntermediateField, at
    FieldTheory/Basic (restrict at :885, lift at :652 as of 2026-08).

  + HOWEVER: The technique needs ALL the per-operation lift lemmas to
    exist for the operations in your identity — a missing lift_sup means
    proving it yourself first. And when a DIRECT lemma for your identity
    exists (e.g. the meet/join idiom in FieldTheory/LinearDisjoint), the
    lift-reflect detour is unnecessary lattice-homomorphism work — search
    for the direct route before committing to the detour.

  + THEN: Apply lift to both sides; rewrite with the per-operation lift
    lemmas until the identity is between ambient objects; close with the
    ambient API; finish by lift_injective. Write it as an explicit calc
    chain — each step names one lift lemma.

  + BECAUSE: Injectivity reflection converts a thin-API setting into a
    rich-API setting at the cost of one lemma per operation — the
    cheapest general exit from subobject-of-subobject reasoning.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Search the namespace for the lift/restrict family by BARE name inside the defining file — qualified-name grep misses namespace declarations.]

