PATTERN. The order lemmas that clear a division or an inverse inside an inequality were generalized from ordered fields to groups with zero, and they now live only under names carrying a subscript-zero suffix. The unprimed spellings a proof recalls from older sources are simply absent for the reals, so the first failure reads as ‘no such lemma’ and sends the seat searching.
Then a second, more expensive thing goes wrong. Every lemma in this family takes a positivity hypothesis for the DENOMINATOR — not for the numerator, and not for the quotient as a whole. Supplying the wrong one does not produce a type error; it produces a rewrite that reports it could not find the pattern, which looks like the name problem again and sends you back to searching a second time. That double-bounce is the actual cost: the fix is to check which side the positivity argument is about BEFORE doubting the name twice.
Practical shape for estimates on an interval: name the positivity facts you will need (the denominator, each endpoint) as hypotheses at the top of the proof and reuse them, because the same strict inequality is consumed by every rewrite in this family and again by positivity and field_simp downstream. Doing this once turns a sequence of stalls into a sequence of one-line rewrites.

LEAF. The forms, all in Mathlib.Algebra.Order.GroupWithZero.Unbundled.Basic, each with the denominator's strict positivity as an explicit argument:
div_lt_iff₀ (hc : 0 < c) : b / c < a ↔ b < a * c;
lt_div_iff₀ (hc : 0 < c) : a < b / c ↔ a * c < b;
div_lt_div_iff₀ (hb : 0 < b) (hd : 0 < d) : a / b < c / d ↔ a * d < c * b;
inv_lt_inv₀ (ha : 0 < a) (hb : 0 < b) : a⁻¹ < b⁻¹ ↔ b < a;
one_lt_inv₀ (ha : 0 < a) : 1 < a⁻¹ ↔ a < 1.
The special case with 1 on the right kept its unprimed name in Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic: div_lt_one (hb : 0 < b) : a / b < 1 ↔ a < b — reach for that one rather than rewriting by hand. Three companions to the same trap: these are iff statements, so use .mp or .2 and do not apply them as functions; 1 / x and x⁻¹ are not interchangeable during elaboration, so state a quantity in the form used by the lemma you are about to compose it with; and after the division is cleared, a nonlinear residue usually needs an explicit product hint such as mul_lt_mul_of_pos_right applied to the hypothesis, because nlinarith on its own will not combine a nonlinear constant bound with an interval bound.
